  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe primary concern for this forecast period will be the impacts \nfrom the remnants of Hurricane Isaias this weekend. The storm \nwill landfall somewhere near the western most panhandle of \nFlorida. The remnants of this storm will move north into western \nand middle Tennessee this weekend.\n\nThe main impacts from this storm across our area will be high winds \nand potential of heavy rainfall for the Plateau, southeast \nTennessee, and southwest North Carolina. Downsloping winds across \nthe central and northern Tennessee valley will limit QPF there.\n\nHere are the more specific impacts from the remnants of Hurricane \nIsaias for Saturday and Sunday:\n\nHeavy Rain Potential: Some tropical bands of heavy rain may move \nacross the Plateau (especially southern), southeast Tennessee, and \nsouthwest North Carolina. Upslope flow into the Plateau will aid \nwith the lift and potential banding. Rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 \ninches are possible. Again, downslope winds will limit QPF amounts \nover the northern and central valley. A flood watch remains in \neffect for parts of southeast Tennessee and southwest North Carolina.\n\nHigh Winds Potential: The first corridor of high wind potential \nexists across SE TN and in and around the Chattanooga area. Since \nIsasia's low is moving a little further west the threat of gusts of \n60 mph has diminished some, however, gusts of 50 mph remain \npossible, especially Lookout and Signal mountains.\n\nThe second corridor and main corridor of high winds will be along \nthe eastern TN mountains and foothills. The REFS continue to depict \nthe potential of high winds with peak gusts up to 70 mph in the \nmountains Saturday. The tight presssure gradient from the wedge over \nthe Carolinas and Isaisa low pressure will produce strong winds.\n\nMountain wave high winds look likely for the far east Tennessee \nmountains and foothills.  A High Wind Watch remains in effect for \nSaturday morning through Sunday morning to address the potential \nhigh winds. Have extended the watch to start at 06Z (2 AM Saturday \nmorning)\n\nFor the rest of east Tennessee, southwest Virginia, and southwest \nNorth Carolina: Surface winds of 15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 45 \nmph are possible Saturday and Sunday. \n\nThe potential for severe weather looks quite limited. Instability is \nlow and mainly elevated especially for Saturday and Saturday night.\nWarm frontal boundary remains south of the area.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, we will see the return of upper level \nridge as it builds into the region. This will return a prolonged \nstretch of dry, mostly sunny weather for much of next week. High \ntemperatures will run 5 to 10 degrees above normal.",
